92186412211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412246 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e78ede4b-d59c-5481-a6c2-71fa96277569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412247 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the mass of a cardboard object which has the form of a quarter-disk, in the second quadrant, of radius $4$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12e862b4-b149-5c0d-8733-0ee2170a820d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412247 #edb/ref 1074 "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $4$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/79/795390e471ee5e96892dfc979f96b803d4a9a4c267410a3afc60a6672f71e63d.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}2\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [2r]_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (8-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(-(-1)+0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412248 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412248 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412248 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412248 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b9/b984f3689ac5191b10a1305408c4888ddbddd4df600060b8d0a84a8e1559fbe1.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the fourth quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Find the mass of the object." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412248 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "474993a9-3dbf-5830-a980-809141314b08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412248 #edb/ref 1074 "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/f2/f2dc1c7b6e558e0f645ad3b6c3d04182554a5778dd7490103bed6ac2e928bcf3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡r]_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412249 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the total mass of the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x=e,$ above the line $y=1$, with mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87aacc28-a327-5afe-ab5f-ccb62ff1e2b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412249 #edb/ref 1074 "The mass of the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $y=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/d5/d55a7318d1d7259c6cb644e05a234fcb2adfbbf7799371c8e11ece4fb8a7150a.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y-1} & =e \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-limits are $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/2a/2afb5f229d56f8b4777ecfe8c1e6b6ba4b414b909cfb0f509cee3c89dac86a84.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type II region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le e,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{y-1})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(1-(y-1))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{y}-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{y}-1)\\text{d}y & =[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the given region is $M=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412250 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the total charge $Q$ of the upper half of the elliptical disk $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$ with charge density function $\\rho $ given by $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{y}{1+x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aeeb285a-1f2b-53b2-a7cc-f9bbdb7632bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412250 #edb/ref 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of an upper half-disk $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y\\ge 0}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{y}{1+x}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\,⟹\\,y\\le 2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/a6/a6f503900a79b3d3c9489f20d6e3bbc46fd848b628d5cf43cca99c14279b542f.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the ellipse with the line $y=0.$ So, the $x$-limits are $-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/91/91abb4000ba8b04d5f7125d20f05dea76f985503b859203a0d63ba7d5c89e37d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}[{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}(\\frac{4(1-{x}^{2})}{2}-0)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2(1-x)(1+x)}{1+x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2x)\\,dx & ={[2x-{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =((2-1)-(-2-1)) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the upper-half elliptical disk is $Q=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412251 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412251 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412251 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412251 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412251 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dcb0881c-0114-5bc7-8c87-99ad233f502c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412251 #edb/ref 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}9{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}48{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}48{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y & =[16{y}^{3}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =16({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=32.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412252 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the total charge $Q$ of a metal plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3303712-2970-5855-8a2d-a322bb15e1d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412252 #edb/ref 1074 "The total charge $Q$ of a plate that occupies the region $D={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express our double integral as a repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{2}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{0}^{4}}{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4{[-\\frac{1}{y}]}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the charge of the plate is $Q=\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
